From a9cda52c00762b740a113e8817659a35573eab59 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: terminills <tdeters@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 10:07:42 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 607/638] Include signal.h in test_launcher.cc instead of
 relying on a transitive one

test_launcher.cc calls kill() and installs a sigaction() handler for
SIGINT/SIGTERM/SIGKILL, but never included <signal.h>. On glibc platforms the
declarations arrive transitively through some other header, so nobody noticed.

On AROS they do not, and the transitive path that happened to supply them
exists only in dcheck_always_on builds. That makes the symptom actively
misleading: the file compiled in the DCHECK build and failed in the no-DCHECK
build of the same tree, with errors that look like a platform gap -

    test_launcher.cc:289: error: 'SIGTERM' was not declared in this scope;
                                 did you mean 'SIPTR'?
    test_launcher.cc:289: error: 'kill' was not declared in this scope
    test_launcher.cc:1116: error: aggregate 'sigaction action' has incomplete
                                  type and cannot be defined

- rather than a missing include. Nothing about AROS is short here: it splits
the declarations across aros/stdc/signal.h (SIGTERM, SIGINT) and
aros/posixc/signal.h (SIGKILL, kill, sigaction, sigemptyset), and the posixc
header includes the stdc one, so one <signal.h> is enough.

Added inside the existing BUILDFLAG(IS_POSIX) include block, next to
<fcntl.h>, since every use of these is already POSIX-only.

This file reaches libcef through //cef:libcef -> //cef:libcef_test_support ->
//net:test_support -> //base/test:test_support, so it is not optional for a
CEF build even though it is test-support code.
---
 base/test/launcher/test_launcher.cc | 10 ++++++++++
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+)

diff --git a/base/test/launcher/test_launcher.cc b/base/test/launcher/test_launcher.cc
index 476caf49dc..c77f335968 100644
--- a/base/test/launcher/test_launcher.cc
+++ b/base/test/launcher/test_launcher.cc
@@ -70,6 +70,16 @@
 
 #if BUILDFLAG(IS_POSIX)
 #include <fcntl.h>
+// This file calls kill() and installs a sigaction() for SIGINT/SIGTERM/SIGKILL
+// but never included <signal.h>; on glibc platforms it arrives transitively.
+// On AROS it does not, and the transitive path that supplied it happens to
+// exist only in dcheck_always_on builds - so the file compiled with DCHECKs on
+// and failed with them off, which is a confusing way to discover a missing
+// include. AROS splits the declarations across aros/stdc/signal.h (SIGTERM,
+// SIGINT) and aros/posixc/signal.h (SIGKILL, kill, sigaction, sigemptyset),
+// and the posixc header includes the stdc one, so this single include is
+// enough.
+#include <signal.h>
 
 #include "base/files/file_descriptor_watcher_posix.h"
 #endif
